.Dd September 26, 2026
.Dt FLATPKG 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm flatpkg
.Nd run Flatpak Linux applications on FreeBSD via jails, unionfs, and the Linuxulator
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Ar options
.Ar command
.Op Ar args ...
.Nm
.Cm run
.Op Ar options
.Ar app-id
.Op Fl Fl Ar args ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a FreeBSD-native front end for running already-deployed Flatpak
applications.
It plays the role Flatpak's own
.Xr bwrap 1 Ns -based
launcher plays on Linux, but builds the sandbox out of
.Xr jail 8 ,
.Xr mount_unionfs 8 ,
and
.Xr mount_nullfs 8
instead of Linux namespaces and OverlayFS, and relies on the Linuxulator
.Pq Xr linux 4
to execute the unmodified Linux ELF binaries that ship inside Flatpak
runtimes and apps.
.Pp
Distribution is delegated rather than reimplemented: the store is a real
OSTree repository driven by the real
.Xr ostree 1
binary, and a per-launch tree is assembled from
.Xr unionfs 8 ,
.Xr nullfs 8 ,
.Xr tmpfs 8
and
.Xr devfs 8
rulesets.
App building
.Xr flatpak-builder 1 Ns -style
and per-syscall filtering
.Xr seccomp 2
are not implemented; the source tree documents the exact boundary and the
gap list against upstream.
.Pp
Every command is served by the system daemon over
.Pa /var/run/flatpkg/control.sock
when it is running.
The client is never setuid: a setuid client would hand the daemon peer
credentials of 0:0 and defeat the group check.
The daemon authenticates the caller with
.Xr getpeereid 2
and runs the application as that user.
Membership of the
.Em flatpkg
group
.Pq see
.Xr pw 8
is what grants access.
.Pp
A location that is
.Em in use
.Pq a store tree a running sandbox has mounted
is never removed: see
.Sx EXIT STATUS
and the
.Cm remove
entry below.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm import Ar path
Scan
.Ar path
for a Flatpak deploy tree
.Pq Pa app/ , Pa runtime/
laid out the way
.Pa ~/.local/share/flatpak
or
.Pa /var/lib/flatpak
are on Linux, and register every app found in
.Nm Ns 's
own store under
.Pa /var/db/flatpkg .
The canonical path is
.Pa /var/lib/flatpkg
.Pq a symlink to the store ;
.Pa /var/lib/flatpak
is a compatibility symlink for Flatpak applications.
.It Cm list
List imported app ids.
.It Cm info Ar app-id
Print the parsed
.Xr flatpak-metadata 5
contents for an imported app.
.It Cm run Op Ar options Ar app-id Op Fl Fl Ar args ...
Build the sandbox root, create a jail branded for Linux compatibility,
and execute the app's
.Cm command=
inside it.
Anything after
.Fl Fl
is passed through to the application as argv.
See
.Sx RUN OPTIONS .
.It Cm install Op Ar options Ar ref ...
Install an application, a runtime, or a
.Pa .flatpak
bundle from a configured remote.
A ref is
.Ql remote:app.id//branch
or the bare
.Ql app.id//branch
when the branch is
.Ql stable .
Pulls the runtime the ref names and any extensions the ref asks for.
The changeset is calculated, shown and confirmed before the store is
touched.
.Pp
The options are
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Fl -runtime
Treat the ref as a runtime rather than an application.
.It Fl -no-pull
Accepted for
.Xr flatpak 1
compatibility; has no effect.
.It Fl -no-deploy
Accepted for
.Xr flatpak 1
compatibility; has no effect.
.It Fl -reinstall
Reinstall the ref even when it is already installed.
.It Fl -or-update
Accepted for
.Xr flatpak 1
compatibility; has no effect.
.It Fl -commit Ns = Ns Ar hash
Install exactly this commit.
.It Fl -from Ns = Ns Ar file
Install from a
.Pa .flatpak
bundle instead of a remote.
.It Fl -y , Fl -assumeyes , Fl -noninteractive
Do not prompt; required when there is no terminal attached.
.It Fl -n , Fl -simulate , Fl -dry-run
Show the changeset and stop before the store is touched.
.It Fl -user , Fl -system
Accepted and ignored: flatpkg has one installation, the system store.
Unlike
.Cm override ,
there is no per-user install to choose between, so the flag selects
nothing rather than doing the opposite of what it says.
.El
.Pp
The following are accepted for parity with
.Xr flatpak 1
and are refused with a diagnostic rather than silently ignored, because
each one would mean the opposite of what it says:
.Fl -no-deps ,
.Fl -no-related ,
.Fl -include-sdk ,
.Fl -include-debug ,
.Fl -no-static-deltas ,
.Fl -subpath Ns = Ns Ar path ,
and
.Fl -sideload-repo Ns = Ns Ar path .
.It Cm uninstall , Cm remove Op Fl -y Op Fl -n Op Ar ref ...
Uninstall a ref.
The changeset is calculated, shown and confirmed before the store is
touched.
A runtime an installed application still needs is refused; flatpkg has no
.Xr flatpak 1 Ns 's
.Fl -force-remove .
A ref a running application has mounted is refused as well.
.Fl -rdepends
also removes every installed ref that depends on the ones given.
.Fl -n
shows the table and stops.
.It Cm update Op Fl -y Op Fl -n Op Ar ref
Update one ref, or every installed ref when none is given.
The changeset is calculated, shown and confirmed before the store is
touched; the last three deploy commits of each ref are kept so that
.Cm rollback
has somewhere to go.
.Fl -y
skips the question and
.Fl -n
stops after the table.
.It Cm upgrade Op Fl Ar options Op Ar ref ...
Re-solve the world file and upgrade the listed refs, or all of them, to
the latest commit a remote offers.
Options:
.Fl -simulate
(also
.Fl -dry-run )
prints the changeset without executing it,
.Fl -prune
drops deploys nothing references any more, and
.Fl -available
limits the solve to what the remotes currently offer.
.It Cm search Ar text
Search the configured remotes, and the installed refs, for
.Ar text .
.It Cm rollback Ar ref Ar commit
Re-check out a previously kept commit.
The valid targets are the ones
.Cm list-commits
prints.
.It Cm list-commits Ar ref
Show the commits
.Cm rollback
can move to.
.It Cm repair Op Ar ref ...
Verify deployed trees and report the suspicious ones.
It is report-only by design: it never deletes or reinstalls anything,
because a repair that repairs is a repair that can destroy a working
tree.
Act on its output with
.Cm install
.Fl -reinstall .
.It Cm history
Print the install, import, upgrade and uninstall log.
An import is recorded as its own action: it is the one install
.Ar flatpkg install
cannot repeat, since the store keeps a reference to a deploy root
rather than a copy of it.
.It Cm ps Op Fl -columns Ns = Ns Ar list
List running instances.
The default is a table; with no
.Fl -columns
the width-adaptive table is used.
.Ar list
is a comma-separated subset of
.Ql instance,pid,application,jail,root .
An unknown component is an error, not an omission: a typo like
.Ql app
for
.Ql application
would otherwise print a table missing the one column the user asked
for, and the empty result looks like a correct answer.
.It Cm enter Ar jid Op Ar command Op Ar args ...
Run
.Ar command
inside the jail of a running instance.
.Ar jid
is a jail id or an instance id, both of which
.Cm ps
and
.Xr sh 1
completion produce.
The command is positional, as it is for
.Xr flatpak 1 ;
a
.Fl Fl
in its place is accepted and dropped.
With no
.Ar command ,
the runtime's
.Pa /bin/sh
is started.
The command runs as the instance's user, or as the caller when
.Ar jid
names no instance.
Only a jail
.Cm flatpkg
created is entered: a bare
.Ar jid
that names somebody else's jail is refused, and says so.
A jail is also entered only by the user it was made for, or by root: the
kernel keeps that user with the jail, and a jail made for another user is
refused even when an instance directory names it.
A jail made before owners were recorded has no owner, and only root may
enter it.
.It Cm kill Ar jid
Terminate a running instance's jail.
.Ar jid
is a jail id or an instance id.
As with
.Cm enter ,
only a jail
.Cm flatpkg
created is terminated; a
.Ar jid
belonging to another jail is refused rather than removed, and so is a jail
made for another user: only that user, or root, may terminate it.
.It Cm exports
Rebuild
.Pa /var/db/flatpkg/exports ,
the farm of
.Pa .desktop
entries and relative symlinks that make apps visible to the host's
desktop.
It also runs after every install and update, and does not touch the
world file.
.It Cm override Op Ar options Ar app-id
Change an application's permissions persistently, in the override file
that Flatseal and
.Xr flatpak 1 Ns 's
.Cm override
write.
The accepted options are
@@OVERRIDE_OPTIONS@@
plus
.Fl -show
to print the file and
.Fl -reset
to empty it.
.Fl -user
and
.Fl -system
select which file is written; the user installation is the default.
.It Cm remote-add Op Ar options Ar name Op Ar url
Add a remote.
.Ar url
may be omitted when
.Fl -from
names a
.Pa .flatpakrepo
file.
Options:
.Fl -from Ns = Ns Ar file
reads the URL from that file, and
.Fl -if-not-exists
makes re-adding a known remote an error instead of a change.
.It Cm remotes , Cm remote-list
List the configured remotes.
.It Cm remote-delete Ar name
Remove a remote.
.It Cm help Op Ar command
Print the main help page, or the page for
.Ar command .
The same bytes are printed by
.Nm
.Fl h
and
.Nm Ar command Fl h .
.It Cm complete Ar what
Hidden helper for shell completion.
.Ar what
is one of
.Cm apps ,
.Cm refs ,
.Cm runtimes ,
.Cm remotes ,
.Cm instances
or
.Cm commands ,
and the output is one bare candidate per line.
.El
.Ss RUN OPTIONS
These are the options
.Cm run
accepts, and they are the whole of it: every one was probed against the
binary, not read off a list.
Grant, policy and environment options that
.Cm run
does not take -
.Fl -nosocket ,
.Fl -nodevice ,
.Fl -nofilesystem ,
.Fl -noshare ,
.Fl -persist ,
.Fl -unset-env ,
.Fl -allow ,
.Fl -metadata ,
.Fl -no-docs ,
.Fl -no-a11y-bus
and the D-Bus name options
.Pq Fl -talk-name , Fl -own-name , Fl -system-talk-name
and friends
-
are
.Cm override
options; set them once with
.Cm override
and they apply to every run of that app.
A
.Fl Fl
ends the option list and everything after it is the application's argv.
@@RUN_OPTIONS@@
.Sh OPTIONS
These are global.  They are recognised before the command, and the ones
that only select a destination or a side effect
.Fl -u Ar name , -user , -system , -no-color , -ostree-verbose , -simulate
are recognised after it too, for compatibility with
.Xr flatpak 1 .
.Fl -version
and
.Fl -help
are not: after a command they belong to the command, and after
.Nm run
they belong to the app.
A
.Ql --
ends the list - everything after it is the command's, and for
.Nm run
the app's.
@@GLOBAL_OPTIONS@@
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev FLATPKG_STORE
Store root.
Ignored when the effective user is root, so that the daemon cannot be
redirected.
.It Ev FLATPKG_DEBUG_KEEP
Set to
.Ql 1
to leave the jail and its mounts in place after the app exits, for
inspection with
.Pa tests/check-clean.sh --clean .
.It Ev FLATPKG_FILECHOOSER_TIMEOUT
Seconds the session portal's fallback
.Em org.freedesktop.portal.FileChooser
dialog may stay open before the portal answers for it.
Defaults to 600; 0 disables the bound.
Used only on a host with no
.Em xdg-desktop-portal
of its own, where the portal owns the desktop portal name itself.
A dialog the user is browsing is not a failure, so the default is
generous; a backend that opens nothing is, and without a bound the
calling app waits for a
.Em Response
that never comes and shows a spinner with no error.
.It Ev FLATPKG_LOGFMT
Append logfmt records to this file, the same as
.Fl l .
.It Ev FLATPKG_OSTREE_VERBOSE
Set by
.Fl -ostree-verbose ;
setting it directly has the same effect.
.It Ev FLATPKG_SECCOMP_ALLOW_CAN , Ev FLATPKG_SECCOMP_ALLOW_BT
Set by the sandbox when the ref asks for
.Ql features=canbus
or
.Ql features=bluetooth ,
to let those socket families through the syscall filter.
An app's own
.Sq Environment
can set them too, so a ref that genuinely needs one is not blocked.
.It Ev FLATPKG_RLIMIT_SHIM_TRACE , Ev FLATPKG_DEVT_SHIM_TRACE , Ev FLATPKG_MEMFD_SHIM_TRACE , Ev FLATPKG_SYNCOBJ_SHIM_TRACE , Ev FLATPKG_MADVISE_SHIM_TRACE
Set one to
.Ql 1
to have that compatibility shim trace what it intercepts, to stderr.
They are four names for one habit: the shims exist because the
Linuxulator cannot express something the app needs -
.Ev RLIMIT_NICE ,
.Ev RLIMIT_RTPRIO
and
.Ev RLIMIT_RTTIME
come back
.Ev EINVAL ;
a compositor
.Vt dev_t
is not the Linux one Mesa compares against;
a
.Vt memfd
has no
.Ev fstatfs ;
and a DRM syncobj wait times out with
.Ev ETIMEDOUT
where Linux says
.Ev ETIME .
Tracing the one you are chasing is faster than adding
.Xr printf 3
to a shim that runs in every app.
.It Ev FLATPKG_SIMULATE
Set by
.Fl -n ;
see the note on that flag for which commands honour it.
.It Ev FLATPKG_NO_SHIMS
Set to
.Ql 1
to skip the
.Ev LD_PRELOAD
compatibility shims.
.It Ev FLATPKG_SECCOMP_DEVEL
Set to
.Ql 1
to disable the syscall filter's ptrace and personality rules.
.It Ev FLATPKG_INTERACTIVE
Set by the daemon worker when the client reported a window size.
Internal.
.It Ev FLATPKG_FORWARD_FDS , Ev FLATPKG_SPAWN_NONCE , Ev FLATPKG_APP_UID , Ev FLATPKG_APP_GID , Ev FLATPKG_APP_USER , Ev FLATPKG_APP_HOME , Ev FLATPKG_APP_MASTER , Ev FLATPKG_APP_TTY
Internal plumbing between the daemon, the portal and the sandbox worker.
.It Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where per-instance directories live.
The daemon worker falls back to
.Pa /var/run/user/<uid>
when it is unset.
.It Ev NO_COLOR , Ev FORCE_COLOR , Ev TERM
Colour control, honoured by
.Nm
itself.
.It Ev FLATPKG_VERBOSE , Ev FLATPKG_LOGFMT
Same as
.Fl v
and
.Fl l .
.It Ev FLATPKG_PROFILE
Same as
.Fl -profile .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Pa /var/db/flatpkg
The store: a real OSTree repository in
.Pa repo/ ,
checked-out deploy trees under
.Pa app/ , Pa runtime/ , Pa extension/ ,
the client state root in
.Pa clients/ ,
where flatpkg's own world file is
.Pa clients/flatpkg/world ,
and the desktop farm in
.Pa exports/ .
.It Pa /var/db/flatpkg/clients/ Ns Ar client Ns Pa / Ns Ar file
State a client owns, such as flatpkg's
.Pa clients/flatpkg/world .
A client declares its files over the control protocol and cannot reach
another client's directory.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/flatpkg/helper.conf
The D-Bus system helper's request map: an absolute command line per
method, with
.Ql %r
the ref and
.Ql %i
the installation; `%b` bundle, `%n` remote, `%o` origin, `%a` arch,
`%p` repo path, `%s` subpaths, `%P` previous ids, `%c` config, `%u` url, `%k`
key, `%v` value, `%S` summary path, `%G` signature path, `%d` source dir
and `%f` flags.  An empty entry is refused; it is not a daemon fallback.
The shipped map enables uninstall, deploy, install-bundle and remote
add/remove.
.It Pa /var/run/flatpkg
The control socket, the daemon's pid file, and one per-launch
union-mounted jail root per running app.
.It Pa /var/run/flatpkg/control.sock
The client-to-daemon socket.
.It Pa /var/log/flatpkgd.log
The daemon's stderr, kept by
.Xr daemon 8 .
.It Pa /usr/local/lib/flatpkg
The
.Ev LD_PRELOAD
compatibility shims: signalfd, seccomp, dmabuf dev_t, memfd
.Pa fstatfs ,
DRM syncobj errno, and rlimit.
.It Pa ~/.local/share/flatpak/overrides/<app-id>
The per-app override file that
.Cm override
writes and Flatseal edits;
.Pa global
in the same directory applies to every app.
The
.Fl -system
files are the same tree under the store,
.Pa /var/db/flatpkg/overrides ,
and the per-app file wins over the global one.
.It Pa ~/.var/app/<app-id>
An application's own writable data, visible inside the sandbox at
.Pa /var/lib/flatpkg/var/data .
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.dbus-proxy
Where
.Xr xdg-dbus-proxy 1
binds the session, system and accessibility bus sockets for a run.
On the host, not inside the jail: the jail root's skeleton is
root-owned and the proxy drops to the caller, so it could not create
sockets there.
The directory is bound read-only into the jail at
.Pa /run/flatpak/.dbus-proxy
since nullfs cannot mount a socket file but can mount the directory
holding it.
Socket names carry the worker's pid, so two concurrent launches of one
app do not unlink each other's socket; the directory itself is reused
and is safe to leave behind.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.flatpkg-p11kit.lock
Lock held while the per-user
.Xr p11-kit 1
trust store is set up.
It lives in the runtime directory rather than under p11-kit's own,
because that directory may not exist yet and must be created with the
user's ownership.
Opened
.Dv O_NOFOLLOW ,
so a user-controlled symlink cannot redirect this root-side open
elsewhere.
.It Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/.flatpak/<id>
Per-instance host directory:
.Pa info ,
.Pa .ref
lock,
.Pa pid ,
.Pa run-environ
(the NUL-separated environment the app ran with, recorded when the
launch reached its final environment - the metadata's
.Sy [Environment] ,
every
.Fl --env=N=V ,
and the sandbox's own
.Ev PATH
and
.Ev XDG_*
pins included;
.Cm enter
restores it, and the four variables a
.Xr jail_attach 2
shell must control are set on top of it)
and
.Pa bwrapinfo.json .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 on a usage or operational error, and otherwise
with the exit status of the application it ran.
.Cm enter
propagates the command's status, and
.Ql 128 + Ns Ar signal
when the command died from a signal.
.Pp
A removal is refused, and the status is 1, when the target is a runtime
an installed app still needs or a store tree a running app has mounted.
Neither refusal is a soft failure that something else goes on to undo.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run an app and let it keep the host's network and its own session bus:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
flatpkg run org.gnome.Fractal
.Ed
.Pp
Run a command inside an app instead of its own entry point:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
flatpkg run --command=/bin/sh org.gnome.Fractal -c 'id'
.Ed
.Pp
Find a running instance, then look at its mounts from inside:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
flatpkg ps
flatpkg enter "$(flatpkg complete instances | head -1)" /bin/sh -c 'cat /proc/mounts'
.Ed
.Pp
Update everything, showing what would change first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
flatpkg upgrade --simulate
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ostree 1 ,
.Xr linux 4 ,
.Xr flatpak-metadata 5 ,
.Xr devfs 8 ,
.Xr jail 8 ,
.Xr mount_nullfs 8 ,
.Xr mount_unionfs 8 ,
.Xr rc 8
.Pp
The design record and the gap list against upstream ship in the source tree.
.Sh BUGS
Report to the flatpkg issue tracker.
Known gaps are listed in the source tree; the ones a user is most likely to
hit first are
per-syscall filtering, which the
.Xr jail 8
facilities do not offer, 32-bit Linux runtimes, and
.Xr flatpak-builder 1 Ns -style
app building.
